WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.710
Hello and welcome back to another
video with ItsDrDan, and today

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:07.080
we're going to be learning about
the strength of acids and bases.

00:00:07.569 --> 00:00:12.390
And the whole idea here is that acids and
bases, they dissociate when dissolved,

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:14.889
when they're added to aqueous solutions.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.140
Now whenever you have acids and bases,
the reason they dissolve is that they

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.300
are considered to be electrolytes.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.310
Now the thing with electrolytes is
that there's two major definitions.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.289
There are strong electrolytes,
and there's also weak.

00:00:28.845 --> 00:00:32.364
Now, strong electrolytes, the most
important thing about them is that

00:00:32.384 --> 00:00:38.374
when they dissociate, they dissociate
completely, meaning that whenever, what

00:00:38.374 --> 00:00:44.344
we mean when we say that is that when
you throw, let's say, HA into solution.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:51.900
It's going to be making all hydrogen ions
and a minus ions when that's thrown into

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.689
solution all Ions when we are here, right?

00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:01.679
There's gonna be no more remnants of the
major molecule When that's thrown in and

00:01:01.679 --> 00:01:06.230
the same thing applies to basis to write
if you throw a strong base Let's say like

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:13.554
hodges like sodium hydroxide You will get
B plus and then all OH minus in solution.

00:01:13.685 --> 00:01:15.655
They completely dissociate.

00:01:16.415 --> 00:01:20.085
Now with weak, they partially dissociate.

00:01:20.454 --> 00:01:22.554
They're still considered acids and bases.

00:01:23.045 --> 00:01:25.265
They still conduct electricity.

00:01:26.035 --> 00:01:29.905
However, it is a very, it's a
lot less because you don't have

00:01:29.905 --> 00:01:33.795
as many positive and negative
cations and anions in solution.

00:01:34.195 --> 00:01:35.605
There's a lot less.

00:01:35.985 --> 00:01:40.925
So when it comes to the solution,
so up above, I use a, I use a giant

00:01:40.925 --> 00:01:42.815
arrow to show complete dissociation.

00:01:43.755 --> 00:01:49.354
up here, even though we still will
write that it makes H plus and a

00:01:49.364 --> 00:01:53.655
minus as it dissociates, there's
not going to be as many solution.

00:01:54.085 --> 00:01:58.075
So you'll notice is that there's
a bunch of H a molecules that

00:01:58.075 --> 00:01:59.965
are still connected together.

00:02:00.364 --> 00:02:02.244
Notice how there's no charge on them.

00:02:02.514 --> 00:02:04.404
They're just in their molecular form.

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:07.964
And then you'll have a
few ions in solution.

00:02:08.264 --> 00:02:10.375
So you still write the same dissociation.

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:13.730
But, there's not going to
be as many in terms of the

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:17.570
concentration of ions in solution.

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:23.430
Now, with this, we essentially as
chemists, we use what we refer to as an

00:02:23.449 --> 00:02:29.659
equilibrium constant, which is called
the acid dissociation constant, to

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.739
tell you how strong or how weak it is.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:37.800
This is the same thing as a K value,
but instead the only difference is that

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.430
we put a little A next to it to tell
you, hey, we're referring to acids.

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:47.300
So there's an acid dissociation
constant, and then there's also a base

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.750
dissociation constant, which is a KB.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:53.540
We typically only really use Ka
though, 99 percent of the time.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.470
So when it comes to Ka's, the
whole idea here is that they

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:59.810
determine the strength of the acid.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:02:59.824
Okay.

00:03:00.205 --> 00:03:04.525
So the bigger the value there is, the
more products, the weak, the smaller

00:03:04.525 --> 00:03:07.265
the value there is, the more reactants.

00:03:07.755 --> 00:03:11.605
So the more H plus ions that are
made, the more electricity that you'll

00:03:11.605 --> 00:03:13.685
have, the stronger it's going to be.

00:03:14.115 --> 00:03:19.115
So using this whole products over
reactants idea, so drill that saying

00:03:19.125 --> 00:03:23.855
in your head, products over reactants,
because when it comes to equilibrium,

00:03:24.275 --> 00:03:27.565
we're going to be saying that quite
frequently when we're trying to write.

00:03:27.885 --> 00:03:29.675
These different equations.

00:03:30.455 --> 00:03:34.965
So when it comes to them being strong,
whether it's a strong acid or a

00:03:34.965 --> 00:03:41.504
strong electrolyte, typically that
Ka is much greater than one because

00:03:41.505 --> 00:03:43.674
there are a whole ton of ions, right?

00:03:43.695 --> 00:03:44.755
All products in there.

00:03:44.755 --> 00:03:46.315
So this number is really, really big.

00:03:46.865 --> 00:03:53.015
And as a result, this number is as tiny as
it can get because you have all products.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.640
Meaning, it's kind of like saying,
what is, uh, 10, 000, uh, 10, 000

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.409
ions compared to one molecule, right?

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:02.749
So it's like a 10, 000 to one.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:04.090
That's huge, right?

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:04.779
As a difference.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.590
Whereas when you have like a weak acid,
well, it's going to be like the opposite.

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:12.320
It's kind of like saying, Hey, we
have one mallet, we have one ion in

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.990
solution and 10, 000 molecules there.

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:18.640
So what's one divided by 10, 000, it's
going to be a really small decimal value.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.990
It's all about the ratios of
how we are looking at these.

00:04:23.549 --> 00:04:25.640
Let's go look at some
different examples of this.

00:04:31.719 --> 00:04:34.830
So when we are writing these, the first
thing we gotta do is understand and

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:38.320
be able to write reversible arrows,
because they allow us to be able

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.650
to represent weak acids and bases.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.370
So let's say if I have an acid
and a base here, so I have HA,

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.209
which is our acid, and water, in
this case, is going to be a base.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:52.840
So that means that HA is going
to donate its proton to water,

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:54.250
so we'll write that out.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:01.140
And as a result, we're going to have A
minus and then H3O plus for hydronium.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.050
Now when we are writing this, there's
a couple notes that need to be made, is

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:08.139
we're going to write our Ka equation.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:13.280
When we are writing them, we
never include, never include

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:17.780
solids or pure liquids like water.

00:05:18.335 --> 00:05:21.354
The reason we don't include
them is that their concentration

00:05:21.364 --> 00:05:23.485
is considered to be constant.

00:05:24.004 --> 00:05:27.474
In terms of water, there's so
much water in solution that it,

00:05:27.604 --> 00:05:31.635
your values won't make any sense
because they don't represent the

00:05:31.635 --> 00:05:33.084
actual chemistry that's occurring.

00:05:33.574 --> 00:05:37.835
They, we only are curious about the
solute or the very tiny piece of it.

00:05:38.485 --> 00:05:40.335
So if we were to write this Ka equation.

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:44.500
The next thing that we got to do
is do products over reactants,

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:45.770
but how do we show them?

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:51.650
Well, we use brackets to indicate
molarity when we are writing these.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:56.809
So every product and every reactants can
be surrounded by square brackets, which

00:05:56.809 --> 00:05:58.919
will indicate how much molarity there is.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.500
Um, so let's do that.

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:05.540
So if we have, let's say a minus,
so we'll put that in brackets.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.849
So products, a minus.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:14.460
and H3O so from our formula on the top,
and that's all going to be divided by the

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.820
reactants, so products over reactants.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:21.490
We don't include water, so we're
just going to write A minus,

00:06:21.879 --> 00:06:26.870
H3O all over HA in brackets.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.930
Now the reason we only include
these other ones is because

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.590
they are all aqueous, right?

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:36.550
So every one of these is aqueous,
And then water is considered to be a

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:37.980
liquid, so we don't write that one.

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:41.539
So now the thing about this, if
you calculate this, so if we had

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:44.390
all the concentrations, we had
them all measured, you usually

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:46.530
would get a Ka that's very small.

00:06:46.549 --> 00:06:50.400
You might get one that's 10 to the
negative 1 to 10 to the negative 14.

00:06:50.409 --> 00:06:53.760
It's usually very, very small
when you're talking about weak

00:06:53.809 --> 00:06:56.440
acids and using Ka values.

00:06:57.965 --> 00:07:02.965
Now, the thing about these though,
the strength and molarity of an

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:05.385
acid determine the acid's Ka.

00:07:05.695 --> 00:07:10.115
Okay, so depending on how strong of an
acid it is and what the concentration.

00:07:10.825 --> 00:07:15.814
Now the thing to keep in mind, an
acid can be considered strong, meaning

00:07:15.844 --> 00:07:23.005
HCl, sulfuric acid, nitric acid, but
it can have a diluted concentration.

00:07:23.475 --> 00:07:26.645
So don't just think like, oh, when
we say something is strong, It

00:07:26.645 --> 00:07:28.835
means that it's like very powerful.

00:07:28.865 --> 00:07:31.805
It could be, you know,
very little molarity.

00:07:32.625 --> 00:07:36.715
And the other thing about it is that
you can also have a weak acid that

00:07:36.725 --> 00:07:39.005
is a, has a very, very high molarity.

00:07:39.015 --> 00:07:40.604
So they're not relevant to each other.

00:07:40.615 --> 00:07:43.265
Molarity and the strength
is what I'm trying to say.

00:07:43.964 --> 00:07:45.894
Let's look at some examples of this.

00:07:45.964 --> 00:07:49.565
All right, so our first one here,
looking at acid base equilibrium.

00:07:50.045 --> 00:07:52.495
The first thing is we got
to write out our reaction.

00:07:53.045 --> 00:07:55.365
So we have HCl reacting with water.

00:07:55.935 --> 00:08:01.484
HCl is a strong acid, so it's going
to be donating its proton to water.

00:08:02.165 --> 00:08:04.435
And as a result, what are
we going to be making?

00:08:04.765 --> 00:08:10.585
Well, we will have Cl minus as one
product, then the other one is going to be

00:08:10.665 --> 00:08:14.874
H3O plus that we're going to make as well.

00:08:15.884 --> 00:08:21.605
Now when we are trying to write
out the, uh, Ka factor, being that

00:08:21.605 --> 00:08:23.725
we were, I gave a value for Ka.

00:08:23.725 --> 00:08:28.055
So with this one, it's a strong acid,
so notice that it's a very big number.

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:28.920
1.

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:32.030
3 times 10 to the 6th power, right?

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:34.830
That's telling you you
have six zeros behind this.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.150
So it's over a million is how big this is.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:38.360
It's very large.

00:08:39.019 --> 00:08:41.379
So what we will do is we'd write 1.

00:08:41.379 --> 00:08:45.989
3 times 10 to the 6th and equal
that two products over reactants.

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:49.260
So what we'll do is we'll
use spur square brackets.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:50.560
We have chlorine ion.

00:08:50.615 --> 00:08:58.855
Then H3O plus ion and then all that's
going to be over HCl and we exclude

00:08:58.855 --> 00:09:03.085
water because water is considered
the solvent and is a pure substance.

00:09:03.704 --> 00:09:05.115
So then that's how you would write that.

00:09:06.384 --> 00:09:10.784
Now the other thing here we
have down below is HF in water.

00:09:11.339 --> 00:09:14.069
Now, the one thing to kind of
point out about these two different

00:09:14.069 --> 00:09:18.020
reactions is notice the arrows
and their difference here, right?

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:22.910
The HCl plus water has a single
arrow, hydrofluoric acid or

00:09:22.939 --> 00:09:25.400
HF has a reversible arrow.

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:31.010
So we use reversible to denote whenever
it's weak in a weak acid or base.

00:09:31.529 --> 00:09:35.400
And we use a single arrow to say,
Hey, we have a strong acid or base.

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:38.689
So it's one way to be able to see
the difference when you see it.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:41.710
So with this one, HF is the acid.

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:42.970
So it donates its proton.

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:48.410
So what we're gonna have is
F minus leftover, and then H

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:51.100
three oh plus as your other ion.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:53.430
If we were to write out the KA.

00:09:54.995 --> 00:10:05.384
So we'd have Ka is equal to F minus, in
brackets, times H3O plus, in brackets,

00:10:05.735 --> 00:10:09.085
all over the concentration of HF.

00:10:09.995 --> 00:10:12.565
And that's how you'd write
out an equilibrium constant.

00:10:13.125 --> 00:10:15.874
And the thing that's important
here is it tells you that being

00:10:15.875 --> 00:10:21.085
that this is A lot less than one
is that it favors the reactants.

00:10:21.495 --> 00:10:25.855
So most of this, of the population
of the solution is going to

00:10:25.855 --> 00:10:27.484
be on the left hand side.

00:10:28.364 --> 00:10:32.285
Whereas when we were looking at the
right one, that one was greater than one.

00:10:33.035 --> 00:10:38.765
So most of that is going to be on the
right hand side of the, of the equation.

00:10:39.355 --> 00:10:40.405
Let's go look at some more.

00:10:40.545 --> 00:10:44.135
Dye and triprotic acids also exist.

00:10:44.185 --> 00:10:48.115
Now these are generally weak acids
that have multiple hydrogens.

00:10:48.555 --> 00:10:52.755
So one example is carbonic
acid, which is a diprotic acid,

00:10:52.945 --> 00:10:55.545
meaning two protons, diprotic.

00:10:56.145 --> 00:10:59.505
So if you have those two
hydrogens, they will dissociate.

00:11:00.194 --> 00:11:04.395
However, when it comes to acids,
you don't rip them all off at once.

00:11:04.615 --> 00:11:07.304
They lose one at a time, right?

00:11:07.304 --> 00:11:10.465
So acids lose one proton at a time.

00:11:10.925 --> 00:11:14.635
That is very important to
realize, is one at a time.

00:11:15.285 --> 00:11:17.655
So here I have H2CO3.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:22.740
yields H plus and HCO3 minus
when you throw it into water.

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:26.819
So how would we write this
in terms of the Ka value?

00:11:27.489 --> 00:11:31.709
So being that it has multiple
protons, you'll kind of notice on

00:11:31.709 --> 00:11:35.210
the right hand or the left hand
side here is that there's a Ka1.

00:11:36.269 --> 00:11:39.070
So it's telling you the
first proton comes off.

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:44.900
So the way that we would say this
is we have Ka1 and we put H plus in

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:49.180
brackets, HCO3 minus in brackets.

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.420
And then the H2CO3 on the bottom.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:54.520
So this would be the first one.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:59.834
Now, being that this has multiple protons,
The thing about it is that, well, the

00:11:59.834 --> 00:12:02.175
reaction could keep going, actually.

00:12:02.175 --> 00:12:03.614
You can actually write more.

00:12:03.614 --> 00:12:08.755
So we can actually take, let's say
if we had a second Ka value, we can

00:12:08.755 --> 00:12:12.594
bring that up here, and we can actually
say, oh, well, what would happen

00:12:12.594 --> 00:12:19.194
if I took, let's say, HCO3 minus,
and I wanted to further dissociate

00:12:19.194 --> 00:12:21.715
this, how would this actually work?

00:12:22.305 --> 00:12:25.615
Well, it would make, essentially, H plus.

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:30.690
And then co minus would be
the other ingredient as this

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:33.140
dissociated even further.

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:34.840
And then we could write Ako.

00:12:36.344 --> 00:12:40.454
Essentially another Ka value for
the next portion and notice that

00:12:40.454 --> 00:12:42.094
the charges change too, right?

00:12:42.124 --> 00:12:46.425
We have a minus and then a two
minus on the next part, right?

00:12:46.454 --> 00:12:51.935
It slowly loses more and more and we have
a different Ka value for the next part.

00:12:52.275 --> 00:12:55.824
Notice how the value is a lot smaller
telling you that this isn't as common.

00:12:55.975 --> 00:13:00.235
There's very little of these products
actually existing in solution.

00:13:00.685 --> 00:13:02.004
This is so carbonic acid.

00:13:02.004 --> 00:13:04.935
So the neat part about it is
this is actually the chemical.

00:13:05.275 --> 00:13:07.935
It helps maintain the pH of your blood.

00:13:08.275 --> 00:13:13.055
So this is what establishes itself to
make sure that your pH does not change

00:13:13.065 --> 00:13:18.304
by very much when you essentially eat
anything or drink anything or anything

00:13:18.305 --> 00:13:21.525
like that gets in contact with your
body that has no harmful effects.

00:13:22.175 --> 00:13:24.465
Let's take a look at some more examples.

00:13:24.615 --> 00:13:30.744
The autoionization of water means that
water can automatically ionize itself

00:13:30.835 --> 00:13:33.414
when it's in a solution of itself.

00:13:34.125 --> 00:13:37.925
So water can act as either an acid
or a base, which is one of the

00:13:37.925 --> 00:13:40.055
most unique characteristics of it.

00:13:40.255 --> 00:13:43.395
It allows it to do all kinds
of chemistry as a result.

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:48.460
Now, being that it can be either,
uh, so anytime you have a substance

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:53.800
that can do both, this is referred
to as amphoteric, meaning it

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:55.760
can do either an acid or a base.

00:13:56.170 --> 00:14:00.689
So if I have, let's say two water
molecules and they want to react

00:14:00.689 --> 00:14:03.590
with each other, which one's
the acid, which one's the base?

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:04.640
Well, we can choose, right?

00:14:04.640 --> 00:14:06.750
It doesn't have to necessarily
be one or the other.

00:14:07.105 --> 00:14:10.165
So let's say the one on the
left is the acid, and then the

00:14:10.165 --> 00:14:11.785
one on the right is the base.

00:14:12.365 --> 00:14:14.825
So what exactly are you
going to make as a result?

00:14:14.865 --> 00:14:19.425
Well, if we donate our proton to the
other one, we are going to pretty

00:14:19.425 --> 00:14:24.124
much have on the left hand side,
what's left is OH minus, and then

00:14:24.124 --> 00:14:30.325
we'll also have H plus as a result,
which if you kind of remember, that is

00:14:30.325 --> 00:14:33.075
your Arrhenius acid base definition.

00:14:33.515 --> 00:14:37.955
Water produces protons,
and it produces hydroxides.

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:41.740
It also follows the Bronsted
Lowry definition, right?

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:45.140
It donated a proton, it
received a proton, it fits both.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:46.170
So they can do that.

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:49.340
So whenever we are looking at some
of these different chemicals, right?

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:53.200
Can we see that, well, when we
have a water, can we have where

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:58.400
one water molecule donates its
proton to the other side and

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:01.260
gives away its proton as a result.

00:15:02.035 --> 00:15:05.295
Alright, so with that being
donated, we have essentially a

00:15:05.295 --> 00:15:08.675
really, really happy water who
has an extra positivity behind it.

00:15:09.005 --> 00:15:12.255
And then a very sad water who
lost its poor little proton.

00:15:12.995 --> 00:15:19.980
So whenever we have an H plus release
by an acid, water will pick it up.

00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:20.630
Okay.

00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:25.270
So one thing about this, that's
really important to realize is

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:30.199
that H plus is generally written to
talk about how acidic something is.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:35.389
We can also use hydronium
ion to represent that too.

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:40.729
So H3O plus, So those two are
generally considered to be equivalent

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:43.020
to one another in solution.

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:46.600
So there's two ways, two different
ways to look at this, right?

00:15:47.379 --> 00:15:54.079
Acids can dissociate to make H plus, or
we can also say that acids increase how

00:15:54.079 --> 00:15:56.969
much H3O plus there are in solution.

00:15:57.679 --> 00:16:01.510
Both tell you that the pH of these
different solutions is going to

00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:05.570
essentially be going lower and lower
and lower, or more acidic as a result.

00:16:06.010 --> 00:16:09.390
because of this increase
in hydrogen concentration.

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:12.490
So they both are equivalent
to each other, which is really

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:13.800
important to write down and note.

00:16:14.470 --> 00:16:15.920
All right, let's take a
look at the next part.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:19.910
Other examples are of polyprotic ions.

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:27.700
Other examples of amphoteric
molecules are polyprotic ions.

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:33.170
So something like HCO3 minus can
behave like both an acid and a base.

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:38.000
So one, so meaning that it either
can donate a hydrogen or receive one.

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:42.570
So for example, if I were to
draw on both sides, the different

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:44.990
types of how can this molecule go?

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:49.940
If I have an additional proton,
we will have carbonic acid, right?

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:51.290
So H2CO3.

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:54.610
And on the other way, if I lose a proton.

00:16:55.310 --> 00:16:58.220
I will have CO3 2 minus.

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:02.090
So with this particular molecule,
it could either receive one

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:04.060
or it could donate one away.

00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:08.870
So when it comes to acid bases, it
depends on how you look at it, right?

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:13.030
If it went this way,
essentially it is receiving one.

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:14.609
So it's actually acting like a base.

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:18.889
If it goes the other way to the
right, it's acting like an acid.

00:17:19.389 --> 00:17:24.589
So, it's all about what direction
do we actually see it going when

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:28.799
it comes to being an acid or a
base or accepting or donating.

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:30.730
Let's look at the next part.

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:33.969
Now the most important part, what
comes with the autoionization of

00:17:33.970 --> 00:17:36.340
water, is all about that relationship.

00:17:36.479 --> 00:17:40.040
It is a Bronsted Lowry acid and base.

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:42.149
It's also an Arrhenius acid and base.

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:46.650
So when it comes to writing the
K factor for this, one thing

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:48.350
we can actually do for this.

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:55.150
Is we can write that K factor
as essentially H plus times OH

00:17:55.400 --> 00:17:57.830
minus and we exclude the waters.

00:17:58.420 --> 00:17:59.990
Now why is that so important?

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:04.899
Is it tells you we have an inverse
relationship, meaning that as H

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:10.320
plus goes up, OH minus goes down and
the opposites occur as well, right?

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:12.929
As the, as the acidity goes down.

00:18:13.334 --> 00:18:15.814
The more alkaline or basic it is goes up.

00:18:16.495 --> 00:18:21.554
So one thing that we can actually do here
is that there is a relationship here at

00:18:21.554 --> 00:18:24.704
room temperature for, for this constant.

00:18:25.215 --> 00:18:27.575
So we call it a KW constant.

00:18:27.585 --> 00:18:29.944
So it's all about water dissociation.

00:18:30.634 --> 00:18:34.325
And what we have here is
H plus times OH minus.

00:18:34.325 --> 00:18:37.945
Those two concentrations
multiplied by each other.

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:39.650
equals 1.

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:42.330
0 times 10 to the negative 14.

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:48.050
So now why is that so big is that no
matter what concentration in solution

00:18:48.050 --> 00:18:54.590
you have of H plus or hydrogen ion
or protons and hydroxide ions, if

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:58.834
you multiply those two concentrations
together, You're always going to get 1.

00:18:58.835 --> 00:19:01.945
0 times 10 to the negative 14.

00:19:02.335 --> 00:19:06.875
So this is a really very important
fact, a formula to realize of what

00:19:06.895 --> 00:19:12.565
the scale is of acidity when it
comes to room temperature solutions.

00:19:12.975 --> 00:19:15.985
And one thing you might realize
is that, okay, I have that 14

00:19:16.005 --> 00:19:17.615
number all the way at the top.

00:19:18.155 --> 00:19:22.275
Well, one thing to think about is
what's the size of the pH scale.

00:19:22.695 --> 00:19:27.475
And you might remember or you're going
to learn about is that the pH scales goes

00:19:27.475 --> 00:19:30.485
from generally about zero to fourteen.

00:19:30.984 --> 00:19:35.175
So what this number is going to lead
to is what your pH scale is, which

00:19:35.185 --> 00:19:38.595
is an easier way to be able to see
how acidic or basic something is

00:19:38.945 --> 00:19:42.325
rather than looking at molarities
and concentrations all the time.

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:43.780
Let's keep moving.

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:47.159
So the next part, realizing
that's to do with solutions.

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:48.950
Let's talk about neutrality.

00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:54.010
Well, if you have a neutral solution, this
is referred to as a pH of seven, right?

00:19:54.010 --> 00:19:55.780
So it's right in the
middle of your pH scale.

00:19:56.170 --> 00:19:57.290
How did they get that value?

00:19:57.300 --> 00:19:58.199
Where does it come from?

00:19:58.830 --> 00:20:02.960
So when, let's say if I had the following
value, where if you have a neutral

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:09.530
solution, that means the acidity is equal
to how basic or alkaline the solution is.

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:12.810
So meaning H plus is equal to hydroxide.

00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:16.410
If they're equal, let's do some simple
math to kind of think about that.

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:17.320
How does that work?

00:20:17.710 --> 00:20:21.440
Well, if they're the same, let's
just use a variable to describe that.

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:25.749
So let's say that I have H plus here
and OH minus, and let's just say that

00:20:25.749 --> 00:20:29.090
they're equal to, let's just say X
being that we're used to algebra.

00:20:29.100 --> 00:20:30.999
We've used X probably most of your lives.

00:20:31.970 --> 00:20:34.040
So if we say, Hey, we have X.

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:38.109
times x and that's equal to 1.

00:20:38.110 --> 00:20:40.490
0 times 10 to the negative 14.

00:20:41.350 --> 00:20:42.470
What's the first thing you can do?

00:20:42.620 --> 00:20:43.860
Let's do x times x.

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:44.819
What do you get?

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:47.139
Well, you'd have squared.

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:50.830
So now if I have x squared and I have 1.

00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:53.579
0 times 10 negative 14,
how do you get x by itself?

00:20:53.700 --> 00:20:54.930
How do you cancel out the square?

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:58.540
Well, the thing you can do is
we can square root it, right?

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:00.179
We can square root both sides.

00:21:00.860 --> 00:21:05.390
So if that's to happen, right, these
cancel out and what you are going

00:21:05.390 --> 00:21:08.150
to get is that x is equal to 1.

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:10.310
0 times 10 to the negative.

00:21:12.079 --> 00:21:16.540
Now why is that so important
is if you think of neutrality.

00:21:17.030 --> 00:21:18.529
So with the seven value.

00:21:19.205 --> 00:21:23.845
One, one of the most important factors
is that if you have a pH equal to

00:21:23.855 --> 00:21:26.565
seven, that's considered to be neutral.

00:21:27.205 --> 00:21:31.334
So let's try to see of what implications
this means in terms of determining

00:21:31.334 --> 00:21:33.114
if something's acidic or basic.

00:21:33.275 --> 00:21:36.635
When we have something that's considered
to be neutral, that is when the

00:21:36.644 --> 00:21:39.245
hydrogen concentration or the protons.

00:21:39.585 --> 00:21:43.745
are equal to the hydroxide
concentration or OH minus.

00:21:44.215 --> 00:21:47.634
So when these are equal, the
concentration of each of them is

00:21:47.634 --> 00:21:52.014
going to be 10 to the negative seventh
power, and that's going to be molarity.

00:21:52.734 --> 00:21:54.094
So that's a pH seven.

00:21:54.655 --> 00:21:59.565
Now, when something is acidic, that means
you have way more protons in solution.

00:21:59.575 --> 00:22:04.584
So we're going to increase that
number and also decrease hydroxide.

00:22:04.655 --> 00:22:06.645
Remember, there's a
relationship as one goes up.

00:22:06.905 --> 00:22:07.675
The other goes down.

00:22:08.495 --> 00:22:12.235
So that means that you're going to
have a bigger number for 10 to the

00:22:12.235 --> 00:22:17.294
minus 10 to the minus seven, like 10
to the minus five, four, three, two.

00:22:17.564 --> 00:22:20.304
So it's getting larger that number, right?

00:22:20.375 --> 00:22:21.495
It's going to be like 0.

00:22:21.495 --> 00:22:22.115
001 0.

00:22:22.154 --> 00:22:25.504
01, much bigger when it's basic.

00:22:25.555 --> 00:22:27.675
That means that that number
is going to be a lot smaller.

00:22:27.685 --> 00:22:30.455
So anywhere from like 10 to
the negative eight to 10 to

00:22:30.455 --> 00:22:32.585
the negative 14, for example.

00:22:33.324 --> 00:22:35.955
So if we were to try to do this
as an example, and I asked you,

00:22:36.065 --> 00:22:37.894
what kind of solution do we have?

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:39.749
If you have 1.

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:46.180
0 times 10 to the negative 4 as your
concentration, is that bigger or smaller?

00:22:46.730 --> 00:22:50.709
Now, for a lot of students, it's
usually really hard to try to understand

00:22:50.709 --> 00:22:52.610
and interpret scientific notation.

00:22:53.180 --> 00:22:56.599
So for this one, if you were to really
think about it, well, how many times

00:22:56.599 --> 00:22:58.600
am I really moving that decimal place?

00:22:59.060 --> 00:23:01.569
Well, it's going to be 0, 0, 0, 1.

00:23:01.950 --> 00:23:04.080
Now, is that bigger or
greater than neutral?

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:06.580
Well, neutral is negative seven.

00:23:06.590 --> 00:23:10.550
So we have one, two, three,
four, five, six, seven.

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:12.360
Which one is larger?

00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:15.769
And you can kind of see, you can see
really quickly that the one on the

00:23:15.770 --> 00:23:21.460
left, which is our original number is
a lot bigger than the other one, right?

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:25.940
It's sometimes that's that, that
power is misleading when you see that.

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:29.060
So what that tells you is
that this is very acidic.

00:23:29.890 --> 00:23:32.509
Now if I asked you a follow up
question, how do you figure out

00:23:32.509 --> 00:23:35.050
what the hydroxide concentration is?

00:23:35.614 --> 00:23:39.745
Well, if you had to do that, we could
rearrange that equation up there

00:23:40.245 --> 00:23:43.834
and we can essentially say that,
all right, well, I have O H minus.

00:23:44.504 --> 00:23:48.005
I'll divide the H plus from
the other side, so we have 1.

00:23:48.005 --> 00:23:54.134
0 times 10 to the negative 14, divide
that by the H plus concentration, and

00:23:54.134 --> 00:23:56.645
we can figure out what the OH minus is.

00:23:56.955 --> 00:23:57.884
So let's plug in our number.

00:23:57.884 --> 00:23:58.625
What would we get?

00:23:59.404 --> 00:24:01.705
So as a result, we would have 1.

00:24:01.705 --> 00:24:05.725
0 times 10 to the
negative 14, divided by 1.

00:24:05.725 --> 00:24:07.405
0 times 10 to the negative 4.

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:11.780
And you'd quickly find out that
that is going to be about 1.

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:16.420
0 times 10 to the negative 10
molarity when it comes to the

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:18.730
concentration of OH minus.

00:24:19.009 --> 00:24:21.540
So notice that it's a
lot smaller of a value.

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:23.019
It's 10 power.

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:25.460
So it should be over inverse relationship.

00:24:25.820 --> 00:24:28.860
And this is how you can try to
solve this particular problem.

00:24:29.430 --> 00:24:30.960
So let's go to our final summary.

00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:33.430
When it comes to trying to
understand the strengths of acids

00:24:33.430 --> 00:24:36.689
and bases, it fully depends on the
level of chemistry you're taking.

00:24:37.475 --> 00:24:40.835
If you're taking just introductory
chemistry, which is just for the beat for

00:24:40.855 --> 00:24:45.175
beginners in chemistry I would recommend
really memorizing your strong acids and

00:24:45.175 --> 00:24:49.605
bases It's a very good starting point
to go same thing if you're in general

00:24:49.605 --> 00:24:54.085
chemistry one as well There are seven
strong acids and then we also have the

00:24:54.085 --> 00:24:59.715
strong bases, which is all of the soluble
hydroxide salts Which are mainly the

00:24:59.755 --> 00:25:05.145
alkali metals Um, so all the alkali metals
and then essentially the first three

00:25:05.255 --> 00:25:07.365
alkaline metals on your periodic table.

00:25:07.995 --> 00:25:12.224
So if you remember those, you
generally will be fine knowing

00:25:12.225 --> 00:25:13.385
the majority of chemicals.

00:25:13.385 --> 00:25:15.665
If you don't see one of
these, that means it's weak.

00:25:16.055 --> 00:25:20.635
About 98 percent of acids and bases
or more are weak acids and bases.

00:25:21.035 --> 00:25:24.305
But if you know these seven, it will
save you a lot of time and frustration.

00:25:24.805 --> 00:25:28.525
So strength is all about looking at
the equilibrium values, which we'll

00:25:28.525 --> 00:25:30.625
